{"parentUuid":"4c6ec20b-60f9-42a5-88f7-c93f205b2da2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"188fe16d-0135-49c2-a1cf-019bf2163090","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MPWCW5uUBN13Xq26ErwKUc","type":"tool_result","content":"155:## Repeating it\n156-\n157-**The page.** Copy \u0060human-probe.html\u0060 and \u0060human-probe.py\u0060 to the Mac, run \u0060python3 human-probe.py\u0060 in a scratch directory, and open \u0060http://localhost:8765/\u0060 in Safari. Find the buttons with \u0060eyes find \"Target A\"\u0060, then run the verbs against them. It logs pointer, wheel and key events, and has a hover-intent menu (\u0060eyes find Menu\u0060; hover it, then find the items) that logs \u0060chosen\u0060 or \u0060ignored\u0060 for each click on an item. The page posts what it saw every half second, and the server appends it to \u0060events.jsonl\u0060, one event a line with the page's own millisecond timestamp. Split pointer events into moves at gaps of more than 300 ms. A move's distance is in screen points from where the cursor rested to the button; the first event a page sees comes after the first jump, so it is not the start. A move's time runs from its first event to its first within 2 points of where it came to rest, and its bow is the furthest any event lies from the straight line between those two rests. A move has covered half its distance at its first event at least half that distance from where it rested. Taken as a share of its time, that is steadier than the time of its fastest frame, which one frame's jitter can move. The page sees what the browser dispatches, about one pointer event a frame, so it understates the event rate of a move that runs longer than a frame.\n158-\n159-**The HID system's timings.** Build \u0060human-hid-params.swift\u0060 with \u0060swiftc -O\u0060. Run as any user, it print\n… 10 more lines","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d45f86e9-10dc-4b43-8595-30a0e18f73b1","timestamp":"2026-10-07T18:02:38.418Z"}
